Q.7 
a) Led the Newness Prioritization feature, optimizing inventory allocation chronologically by floor-set data to ensure earlier fulfillment, 
projecting a 5% increase in store sell-through rate and a reduction in markdown risk.
Ans One of the problems we had was around new products or newness. Different products have different floor-set dates, which means the business want 
certain products to reach stores earlier based on when they are supposed to be launched on the sales floor.
I worked on a Newness Prioritization feature where we used floor-set dates to prioritize inventory allocation. 
Instead of treating all products equally, we prioritized products with earlier floor-set dates so that inventory reached the right 
stores in time for the planned launch. This helped improve the alignment between inventory availability and the planned selling calendar. 
We projected around a 5% improvement in store sell-through and also expected to reduce markdown risk because products were available 
closer to their intended selling period.

b) Why does this reduce markdown?
"If a product reaches the store late, we lose part of its selling window. That can result in slower sales and eventually markdowns to clear the inventory. 
By prioritizing products according to their floor-set dates, 
we improve the probability that the inventory is available when customers are expected to buy it."

c) Minimized stockouts by 10% through the implementation of King's Formula for safety stock calculation...
"One of the inventory problems we were trying to solve was stockouts. The challenge was that simply using average demand wasn't sufficient
because both demand and lead time could vary.
I worked on implementing King's Formula for safety stock, where we considered demand variability as well as lead-time variability. 
The objective was to determine a more appropriate buffer inventory rather than using a fixed safety-stock number.
We then used that safety stock as part of the replenishment and inventory planning logic. This helped reduce stockouts by approximately 10%."

"Why do we need safety stock?"
"Because actual demand and lead time aren't perfectly predictable. Safety stock acts as a buffer against that uncertainty so that inventory 
doesn't run out before replenishment arrives."

"Why consider lead-time variability?"
"Even if demand is stable, if suppliers or distribution centers sometimes take longer to replenish, we need additional 
buffer inventory to protect against that uncertainty."

d) Enhanced store-level inventory accuracy by 12% by implementing Core vs. Fashion product segmentation...
We realized that different products behave differently from an inventory perspective. Core products have relatively stable and 
predictable demand because they are continuously sold, whereas fashion products tend to have more variable and seasonal demand.
So instead of applying the same safety-stock logic to all products, I worked on segmenting products into Core and Fashion based on sales 
consistency and demand behavior.
We then used Pareto analysis to understand which products contributed most significantly to sales and adjusted inventory 
and safety-stock logic accordingly.
This improved the accuracy of store-level inventory planning by around 12%

"Why shouldn't you use the same safety-stock approach for Core and Fashion?"
"Because their demand patterns are different. Core products typically have more stable demand and can support more reliable historical estimates,
whereas fashion products can have higher volatility, shorter life cycles and stronger seasonality. 
Therefore, applying the same inventory assumptions can lead to overstocking or stockouts."

